<!-- section: hero -->

# A 90° Antenna Rotation, a 3 dB Difference

**See how antenna orientation changes reception.**

Keep one antenna fixed, rotate the other, and follow the difference. This 160 m experiment shows how to find a change in WSPRadar, check it on one radio path, and see what the decoded signals add to the story.

<!-- section: summary -->

## The findings at a glance

- [**Change on one path**](#stations)

    <strong class="case-finding-value">3.0 <span>dB</span></strong>

    M7AEO's reconstructed median changes from **−0.6 to +2.4 dB**, favoring the fixed long wire relative to the rotated dipole.

- [**Across station results**](#difference)

    <strong class="case-finding-value">+1.4 <span>dB</span></strong>

    The full-period station-balanced median, based on **5 367 qualifying Joint Spots from 50 transmitting-station entries**.

- [**More decoded signals**](#decoding-reach)

    <strong class="case-finding-value">1.29<span>×</span></strong>

    **7 054 versus 5 489** across the full period: **1 565 additional decodes** at the long-wire setup.

The whole-period results combine both antenna orientations. Follow the time charts to see the change associated with rotation.

<!-- section: setup -->

## What were the operators testing?

Can turning a receiving antenna through 90° make a visible difference in WSPR results?

Jurgen Vanhamel, Walter Machiels and Hervé Lamy compared two receiving setups on 160 meters:

- **ON4AWM0 — Target:** an ITA LWA II long-wire antenna, kept in the same orientation throughout the experiment.
- **ON4AWM1 — Reference:** an adapted shorted dipole, initially parallel to the long wire and then rotated 90° in the horizontal plane after the first week.

WSPRadar matches reports of the **same transmitter and reported locator in the same two-minute WSPR cycle**. These <strong class="defined-term">Joint Spots</strong> let us compare the two setups at the same time. A <strong class="defined-term">Target-only</strong> observation means only ON4AWM0 reported that transmitter-cycle; <strong class="defined-term">Reference-only</strong> means only ON4AWM1 did.

<strong class="defined-term">Signal-to-noise ratio, or SNR,</strong> describes how strongly a signal stands out relative to noise. Higher SNR is better. Here:

**ΔSNR = ON4AWM0 SNR − (ON4AWM1 SNR + 1.6 dB)**

**Positive values favor the long-wire setup; negative values favor the dipole setup.** The dipole is the antenna that changes; the long wire stays fixed.

The prepared correction accounts for an established receiving-chain offset. The companion [**One Antenna, Two Receivers — Why Calibration Matters**](/en/demos/vanhamel_rx_calibration/) explains that check. A constant correction shifts the SNR differences without changing the size of a before-and-after step.

**Our question is: where, when and by how much did relative reception change—and did one setup also decode more transmissions?**

<!-- section: load-demo -->

## Load the demo and check its settings

Select **Run in WSPRadar App**, review the prepared settings, then select **Run Analysis**.

| Setting | Value in this demo |
| :--- | :--- |
| Analysis | **RX Benchmark → Reference Setup/Station** |
| Target / Reference | **ON4AWM0, JO20OT / ON4AWM1, JO20** |
| Band | **160 m** |
| UTC window | **1 May 2021, 17:15 → 15 May 2021, 07:00** |
| Reference SNR Correction | **+1.6 dB — established offset** |
| Distance / solar state | **Within 5 000 km / All** |
| ΔSNR support | **≥3 Joint Spots per station; ≥3 qualifying stations per map segment** |
| ΔDecode map support | **1% of the run's observed events per segment** |
| Chronological charts / station to select | **12h / M7AEO at IO82** |

Follow **Global View → Segment Inspector → Station Insights → Drill-Down**, then **Download Evidence**. Start with **Full Range | All Directions** in the Inspector.

Each station needs **at least three Joint Spots over the comparison period** to qualify for ΔSNR, and each colored map segment needs **at least three qualifying stations**. This reduces reliance on just one or two observations within a station, or just one or two station results within a segment. The segment minimum is **three rather than the default of five** to retain more directional coverage in this sparser 160 m dataset. The ΔDecode map uses a separate count requirement, explained below; its pooled decode counts are unaffected by these ΔSNR minimums. Neither display threshold is a test of statistical significance.

<!-- section: delta-snr-map -->

## Read the ΔSNR Map: does every direction agree?

Open **Global View → ΔSNR Map**. The map is centered on ON4AWM0. Compass sectors show direction from the Target; rings separate distance ranges.

For each transmitter, WSPRadar calculates its **median ΔSNR**: the middle difference when its Joint Spots are sorted. It then takes the median across those station results within each segment. **Every qualifying station has one equal voice**, whether it contributed three pairs or hundreds. Frequently heard transmitters cannot dominate the map simply by supplying more reports.

| Geographic scope | Station-balanced ΔSNR | Qualifying stations |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| **0–2 500 km, ENE** | **+1.4 dB** | **3** |
| **0–2 500 km, WNW** | **+2.4 dB** | **14** |
| **0–2 500 km, SSE** | **−3.6 dB** | **3** |

The long wire leads on the sampled ENE and WNW paths; the dipole leads in SSE. **An overall advantage does not describe every direction.**

Select a direction and distance in **Segment Inspector** to examine its stations and counts. Gray hatching means observed evidence below the map's support requirement; blank segments have no observed station entries. The neutral color spans a range around zero. Read the numerical scale rather than treating every pale sector as an exact tie.

Pale-green station markers have Joint evidence but fewer than three Joint Spots. These stations do not contribute to the ΔSNR summaries; their observed decodes remain included in ΔDecode.

This map combines both weeks. It shows the overall directional comparison, not the change caused by rotation. Return to **Full Range | All Directions** before continuing.

<!-- section: decoding-reach -->

## Open Decodes & Stations by SNR: what else was received?

Move to **Global View → Decodes & Stations by SNR**. Follow the curves **from stronger signals on the left toward weaker signals on the right**. Each step includes reports down to a lower SNR cutoff. The upper row counts spots; the lower row counts distinct station entries, meaning callsign plus reported locator.

The **Joint** curves both finish at **5 379 spots from 60 stations**. Their separation before the endpoint reflects different reported SNRs for the shared transmissions; it does not represent extra decodes. These curves include all retained Joint Spots, including those from stations below the three-pair ΔSNR minimum.

The upper-middle panel adds one-sided observations, which cannot supply paired ΔSNR. **The long wire records far more of these, mainly toward the weak-signal end.** This is where extra reception margin can help a signal produce a report. The same transmitter can contribute Joint Spots in one cycle and a one-sided report in another.

Read the right-hand endpoints as the observed totals:

| Observed result | Long wire · ON4AWM0 | Dipole · ON4AWM1 |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| Joint Spots | 5 379 | 5 379 |
| One-sided spots | 1 675 | 110 |
| **Total decodes** | **7 054** | **5 489** |
| **Distinct station entries** | **73** | **62** |

The long wire recorded **1.29× as many decodes**, about **29% more**. It also heard **13 station entries that the dipole never reported**; the dipole heard **two that the long wire missed entirely**. More decodes and more stations are different benefits: repeated reception of a familiar transmitter increases the spot count without adding another station.

These curves use each receiver's **raw reported SNR**. The +1.6 dB correction does not move them or change any decode counts. A one-sided report supplies no measured SNR for the receiver that missed it.

<!-- section: delta-decode-map -->

## Read the ΔDecode Map: where were more signals decoded?

Open **Global View → ΔDecode Map**. Each receiver's total is **Joint + its own one-sided decodes**. WSPRadar pools those counts across the selected segment; it does not average station ratios.

In **WNW at 0–2 500 km**, the totals are **2 143 for ON4AWM0 and 1 866 for ON4AWM1**:

**2 143 ÷ 1 866 = 1.15× as many long-wire decodes**, a net **277 additional decodes**.

Compare the same three directions with the SNR map:

| Scope, 0–2 500 km | Paired ΔSNR | Decode advantage |
| :--- | ---: | :--- |
| **ENE** | **+1.4 dB** | **Long wire 1.03×** |
| **WNW** | **+2.4 dB** | **Long wire 1.15×** |
| **SSE** | **−3.6 dB** | **Dipole 1.75×** |

**ENE has a positive paired-SNR margin but nearly equal decode totals.** WNW has both a larger paired-SNR margin and a larger decoding advantage. In SSE, both measures favor the dipole. Better SNR can provide additional margin even when both setups already decode most of the observed transmissions. The two measures also weight the evidence differently: qualifying station medians for ΔSNR, all retained decode counts for ΔDecode.

On the legend, **2× means twice as many decodes for the indicated setup**. Warm colors favor Target; blue colors favor Reference. Here, the central light-green band extends to the **1.1× boundaries in either direction**, so it is not necessarily a tie. The scale adapts to the results.

A segment needs **72 observed events** to receive a color: 1% of the run's **7 164 transmitter-cycle events**, rounded up, with each Joint event counted once. Hatching means insufficient volume for the map, not equal reception; the evidence remains available to inspect.

**Interpret decoding differences under comparable operation and reporting.** An outage or missing upload can also remove a report. Transmissions missed by both receivers remain unknown, so ΔDecode does not measure absolute decoding probability.

<!-- section: difference -->

## Read Benchmark Statistics: how large is the overall difference?

Open **Segment Inspector → Benchmark Statistics** with **Full Range | All Directions** selected.

The three panels summarize different aspects of the same comparison:

- **ΔDecode — Decode Volume:** stations are arranged by their decode advantage. Bar height shows their share of receiver decodes, not their share of stations. The dashed line marks the pooled **ON4AWM0 1.29×** result. The **Exclusive** endpoints contain decodes from stations heard only by one setup, not every one-sided spot.
- **ΔSNR — Qualifying Joint Spots:** every paired observation from a qualifying station contributes: **5 367 pairs** in total. The median is **+2.4 dB**, so frequently heard transmitters have more influence.
- **ΔSNR — Qualifying Stations:** each of the **50 qualifying stations** contributes one median. The station-balanced result is **+1.4 dB**.

**50 of the 60 stations with Joint evidence meet the three-pair minimum.** The other ten contribute just 12 pairs between them. Their reports remain in the decoding views but are excluded from the qualifying ΔSNR distributions. This explains the small difference between the Joint-Spot totals in the cumulative curves and the ΔSNR histogram.

The two SNR summaries both favor the long wire, but describe different typical results. **They are not an uncertainty range**, and equal station weight does not mean equally strong supporting evidence. In Station Insights, compare **Joint Spots** with **Median ΔSNR** to see how much data supports each station result. The gap between the two medians alone does not identify which paths or periods produced it.

Both distributions also extend below zero: some comparisons favor the dipole. Most importantly, **neither full-period median measures the rotation effect**. For that, follow the comparison through time.

<!-- section: time -->

## Read ΔSNR Temporal Coverage: when did reception change?

Open **Segment Inspector → ΔSNR Temporal Coverage**.

### Follow the experiment on the left

Each circular marker is the median of the Joint Spots from qualifying stations in one **12-hour interval**. Look for the upward change around **8 May**. Several well-populated early intervals lie near **−0.6 dB**; later intervals often lie around **+2.4 to +4.4 dB**. Relative reception has shifted toward the fixed long-wire setup.

The dashed line is the overall **+2.4 dB Joint-Spot median**. The shaded band contains the **middle 50% of observations**, showing spread rather than a confidence interval. Background colors show where observations concentrate; they do not indicate which antenna is better. Read the labeled dB values because the vertical scale is nonlinear.

This view pools individual observations, so frequently heard transmitters carry more weight. Next we will check whether the change also appears on one path.

### Compare hours of the day on the right

The UTC-hour panel combines the same hour across dates. Most paired reception occurs in the evening, night and early morning; daytime coverage is sparse. This is consistent with the strong day–night changes in 160 m ionospheric propagation.

The hourly profile combines **both antenna orientations**. Use it to explore the daily pattern; use the chronological panel to follow the rotation experiment.

<!-- section: evidence -->

## Read ΔDecode Temporal Coverage: check the counts behind the pattern

Open **Segment Inspector → ΔDecode Temporal Coverage**. This view shows both the supporting reception and the advantage in the number of stations or transmissions decoded.

The **upper row counts distinct stations within each time bin**. Green **Both observed** means each receiver heard that station at least once in the bin; those receptions need not be in the same cycle. Magenta and cyan show stations heard only by Target or Reference in that interval.

The **lower row counts transmitter-cycle events**. Here green means **Joint**, requiring the same WSPR cycle. Magenta is Target-only; cyan is Reference-only. Extra decodes of an already-heard transmitter can enlarge the lower bars without increasing the upper station count.

Read the overlaid lines against the **right-hand axes**: **1× means equal totals; above favors Target; below favors Reference**. The upper line compares distinct-station totals; the lower line compares total decodes. The right-hand panels show average counts by UTC hour, with the advantage calculated from the combined counts.

The repeated overnight coverage supports the broad SNR pattern. Daytime ratios can look dramatic while the bars contain very few observations. For example, the all-station SNR point at **13:00 UTC is +7.4 dB—but rests on just one Joint Spot across the whole run**.

The three-Joint-Spot station minimum applies across the full comparison period, so an individual time bin can still contain only one pair.

**Check the count behind a striking value.** A large ratio on a tiny bar and a repeated advantage across hundreds of comparisons carry different amounts of evidence.

<!-- section: stations -->

## Open Station Insights: follow the rotation on one path

Select **M7AEO at IO82**. It is about **598 km away at 291°** and supplies **1 441 Joint Spots**.

The station list covers the full analysis by default. **Filter by selected segment and distance** narrows the list; the selected station's evidence still covers its full run. **Include one-sided evidence** also lists stations without Joint Spots.

### Follow M7AEO's ΔSNR

Open its **ΔSNR Temporal Coverage** chart.

Most early 12-hour medians are below zero. After the change around **8 May**, they become positive; many later intervals settle around **+2.4 dB**. The change remains when we follow one transmitter, so a changing mix of transmitters cannot explain the entire pooled pattern.

Using the observation boundary from the published Figure 6 reconstruction:

| M7AEO observations | Median ΔSNR |
| :--- | ---: |
| **First 750 Joint Spots** | **−0.6 dB** |
| **Remaining 691 Joint Spots** | **+2.4 dB** |
| **Change** | **+3.0 dB** |

The full-period M7AEO median is only **+0.4 dB**. That single number hides the transition.

**Keep the sign in mind: the fixed long wire becomes stronger relative to the rotated dipole. This is not a 3 dB improvement for the antenna that was turned.**

<!-- section: station-decodes -->

## Then check M7AEO's decodes

Open its **ΔDecode Temporal Coverage** chart. Across the run, this path supplies **1 550 long-wire decodes versus 1 448 dipole decodes**, or **1.07× as many at the long-wire setup**.

The large overnight bars contain mostly Joint reception. Both setups often decoded the same transmissions, even while their relative SNR changed. A better SNR margin need not produce a proportional increase in decodes.

For a concrete contrast, the **00:00–01:00 UTC** bin, pooled across the run, has **+1.4 dB paired ΔSNR** but **138 decodes at each receiver: 1×**. Here the long wire supplied extra SNR margin without additional observed decodes.

Check sparse intervals too: the **9 May, 05:15–17:15 UTC** bin has a **+4.4 dB median from one Joint Spot**. The broad transition rests on the repeated observations, not on that isolated high point.

The **1.07×** total combines both orientations; it does not measure how much the rotation itself changed decoding.

<!-- section: published-figure6 -->

## Compare with the published experiment

The original **Figure 6**, the reconstruction and WSPRadar's M7AEO chart show the same upward transition. The publication plots successive receptions; WSPRadar groups observations by UTC time. Different horizontal axes reveal the same change in relative reception.

[**Explore Figure 6, the reconstruction and WSPRadar comparison ↗**](/static/reference_figures/vanhamel_fig6_rotation_v1/WSPRadar_Demo_Vanhamel_Figure6.pdf)

The split after **750 Joint Spots** follows the publication's reconstruction boundary. It is **not an independently logged UTC rotation time**. The new ΔDecode views add a reception-count perspective; they are not a claim that the paper published the same decoding analysis.

*Calibration note:* This demo uses **+1.6 dB**, rounded from **1.57 dB** documented on the [authors' website](https://sites.google.com/myuba.be/wspr-station-compare/home/wspr-antenna-comparison-instrument). The paper reports **+1.2 dB**, as examined in the companion calibration demo. Using +1.2 dB here would move every paired difference **0.4 dB higher**, but leave the **3 dB step unchanged**. Agreement with the reconstructed figure does not establish which correction the authors used to draw it.

<!-- section: drill-down -->

## Use Drill-Down: check an individual comparison

With **M7AEO** selected, open **Drill-Down** and compare the timestamp and SNR columns:

| UTC time, May 2021 | ON4AWM0 SNR | Corrected ON4AWM1 SNR | ΔSNR |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| **1 May, 18:24** | −20.0 dB | −19.4 dB | **−0.6 dB** |
| **7 May, 20:04** | −12.0 dB | −12.4 dB | **+0.4 dB** |
| **8 May, 20:16** | −16.0 dB | −20.4 dB | **+4.4 dB** |
| **14 May, 20:04** | −17.0 dB | −20.4 dB | **+3.4 dB** |

The first comparison favors the dipole. On 8 May, **−16.0 − (−20.4) = +4.4 dB**, favoring the long wire. These processed SNR values use a common **1 W (30 dBm) reported-power basis**. The Reference column also **already includes the +1.6 dB correction**; do not add it again. The earlier cumulative curves use raw reported SNR. The common power adjustment on both sides leaves ΔSNR unchanged.

Use **Zoom window** to focus the plots and table; **Filter table** changes only the displayed rows. You do not need to inspect thousands of rows: use this view to verify the arithmetic or investigate a surprising point. The before-and-after result comes from the full observation groups, not from choosing two individual rows.

<!-- section: takeaways -->

## Download Evidence: take the method to your own station

On the M7AEO path, **1 441 same-cycle comparisons reveal a 3 dB upward shift associated with rotating the Reference dipole**. Across both orientations and all sampled paths, the long-wire setup has a **+1.4 dB station-balanced SNR advantage** and **1.29× as many observed decodes**. The maps show where those advantages hold—and where the dipole leads.

These are results for the two installed receiving setups and the sampled paths. They do not establish a 3 dB antenna-gain change in every direction.

For your own antenna experiment, use the same sequence: **find the difference on the maps, follow it through time, check a well-observed transmitter, then inspect the underlying reports**. Keep notes on antenna orientation, the time of each change, receiver settings and common operating periods.

Under **Download Evidence**, select **Prepare All Results for Download** to preserve the configuration, figures and processed observations. **Save Config** separately keeps the reusable analysis settings.

You have followed a physical antenna change down to individual WSPR reports—and learned to distinguish **a better SNR margin, additional decoded transmissions and additional stations heard**.

